    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1003">

                <pb facs="1253" n="1243" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg1243"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/12–2346: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Major General Donald H.
                            Connolly to Mr. John B. Stetson,
                            Jr.
                    </hi><anchor n="10" xml:id="d1003-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-12-23">23 December 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>88373. 1. Arrangements now being completed<anchor n="11" xml:id="d1003-f2"/> for
                    establishment by the US Treasury of two funds for 25 million dollars and 5
                    million dollars respectively to the credit of the Government of the Republic of
                    China for the fulfillment of the purposes as provided in Article 3 of August 30
                    Agreement. 2. Consideration being given to the proposal for a revolving fund to
                    be placed in the custody of Bosey<anchor n="12" xml:id="d1003-f3"/> expendable
                    by Bosey with withdrawals replaceable as the appropriate <pb facs="1254"
                        n="1244" xml:id="pg1244"/> required documentation is supplied by Bosey. 3.
                    Meanwhile we suggest Bosey advance such funds as necessary to avail and expedite
                    movement of surplus with the assurance hereby given that Bosey will be promptly
                    reimbursed upon receipt of certifying documents. 4. It is suggested that the
                    exact form of documentary requirements for payment or reimbursement await
                        Kendall<anchor n="13" xml:id="d1003-f4"/> and Stetson’s arrival Shanghai
                    early January as well as determination as to the constitution and function of a
                    Board of Review at Shanghai as suggested paragraph 3 our cable of 9
                        November.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d1003-f5"/> 5. Suggest you share the above
                    with Bosey and advise soonest whether these arrangements and assurances remove
                    all obstacles in way of expeditious procedure.</p>
                <closer>[File copy not signed]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1003-f1" n="10" type="source" xml:id="d1003-f1-content">
                <p>Central Field Commissioner, Pacific and China, OFLC, at Manila.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1003-f2" n="11" xml:id="d1003-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 89641, January 14, 1947, Maj. Gen. Connolly informed the OFLC
                    Shanghai Office that agreement had been reached with the War and Treasury
                    Departments on immediate establishment of the two funds.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1003-f3" n="12" xml:id="d1003-f3-content">
                <p>Board of Supplies, Executive Yuan, the Chinese agency charged with taking
                    possession of and distributing surplus property.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1003-f4" n="13" xml:id="d1003-f4-content">
                <p>Charles B. Kendall, Deputy Director, Legal Division, OFLC.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1003-f5" n="14" xml:id="d1003-f5-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
